    "bottomLine": "If you already own a power station, a plain 20-inch box fan is the most air per watt-hour you can buy for a summer outage.",
    "description": "This is not a battery fan, and that is the point. A 20-inch box fan moves a large volume of air with a simple motor, and box fans of this size commonly draw well under 100 watts even on high; check the label on yours for the exact figure. The Lasko 3733 is a typical three-speed model, sold in nearly every hardware store. Paired with a power station, it becomes a cordless room fan that outperforms every battery fan here on airflow. A 1kWh station running a box fan at around 60 watts would last in the range of 12 to 14 hours once inverter losses are counted, which covers a full night with plenty left for phones. Smaller stations work too; a 300Wh unit still runs one for several hours. The inefficiency is the inverter, which uses some power just to stay on, so a DC fan wins on small batteries. On a mid-size or larger station, though, the box fan is the best value cooling you can buy."
