    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The best battery powered fan is usually the one that runs on a battery you already own. If you have a drawer of Ryobi, DEWALT or Milwaukee packs, a bare-tool fan is the cheapest, longest-running option there is. If you do not, a fan with a built-in battery and USB-C charging is simpler, and for long outages a plain box fan on a power station still moves the most air per dollar.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="what-matters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Actually Matters in a Battery Fan
          </h2>

          <p>
            Fan listings love airflow claims, speed counts and wind-chill graphics. Most of that matters less than one boring fact: how much energy the battery holds, and how easily you can replace or recharge it. A fan with a beautiful design and a small, sealed battery that dies at 3 a.m. is worse than a plain fan that slides onto a battery you have three spares of.
          </p>

          <p>
            Battery fans fall into four families. Tool-battery fans run on the same packs as your drill, so they cost little if you are already on a platform and can run for days if you have several packs. Built-in battery fans charge from the wall or USB-C and suit people without tool batteries. D-cell fans run on disposable batteries that store for years, which makes them ideal for emergency kits. And ordinary AC fans run from a power station or generator, moving the most air for the money if you already own one of those.
          </p>

          <p>
            Size and noise come next. A 7-inch personal fan cools one person at a bedside or desk and can be quiet enough to sleep beside. An 11-inch jobsite fan or a 20-inch box fan moves enough air to change how a room feels but makes more noise and draws more power. Decide whether you are cooling a person or a space before you shop, because a fan that tries to do both usually does neither well.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, think about the scenario. A summer outage, a weekend at a hot campsite and a day in a sweltering attic are different problems. For outage planning, the fan is one part of a kit that also includes lights and phone power, which our guides to the <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">best portable power stations</a> and the <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">best home generators for power outages</a> cover. For camping and jobsites, weight, toughness and battery sharing count for more.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Seven Best Battery Powered Fans
          </h2>

          <p>
            These are grouped by power source rather than ranked on a single scale, because the right fan for a contractor with a dozen 20V packs is the wrong fan for a family stocking an outage kit. We have not lab-tested these fans; specifications are as each manufacturer lists them, and runtime estimates are worked from battery capacity rather than measured. Tool-only fans are sold without a battery or charger, so factor that in if you are not already on the platform.
          </p>

