        <div id="runtime" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Runtime vs Battery Size: The Math That Matters
          </h2>

          <p>
            Runtime claims on fan boxes are usually measured on the lowest speed with the biggest compatible battery, so they set expectations you will rarely see in practice. The honest way to estimate runtime is to work it out yourself. Multiply battery voltage by amp-hours to get watt-hours, then divide by the fan's power draw at the speed you will actually use.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Battery Energy by Pack (Calculated)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">36 Wh</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">12V 3Ah pack</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">72 Wh</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">18V 4Ah pack</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">100 Wh</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">20V MAX 5Ah pack</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~18 Wh</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">5,000mAh handheld cell</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Tool fans in the 7- to 11-inch class typically draw somewhere in the range of 10 to 40 watts depending on speed and size, with the low setting near the bottom of that range. On that basis, a 72-watt-hour 18V pack runs a small fan for roughly six or seven hours on low and two or three on high. A 100-watt-hour 20V MAX pack running a larger jobsite fan on medium lands in a similar range. These are planning estimates; the fan's own manual or listing is the place to check specific runtime figures.
          </p>

          <p>
            Handheld fans work on the same math at a much smaller scale. A typical handheld lithium cell rated at 5,000 milliamp-hours stores about 18 watt-hours at its 3.7-volt nominal voltage. A small fan drawing a few watts runs for hours on low but drains quickly on its top speed, which is why handheld runtime claims vary so wildly between speeds. If a listing gives battery capacity only in milliamp-hours, multiply by 3.7 and divide by 1,000 to get watt-hours.
          </p>

          <p>
            Speed is the biggest runtime lever. Fan power rises steeply with speed, so dropping from high to medium often stretches runtime far more than you would expect from the change in breeze. At night, the lowest speed pointed directly at the bed is usually enough, and it can make a single pack last until morning. If you need to recharge packs or handheld fans during a long outage, a <a href="/best-solar-power-bank/">solar power bank</a> or a small panel keeps USB devices topped up, and most tool brands sell chargers that run from a power station.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tool-platforms" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Tool-Battery Platforms: Ryobi, DEWALT and Milwaukee
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you already own cordless tools, a bare-tool fan is the best value in this category. The fan itself costs little because the expensive part, the battery, is already on your shelf. You also get the platform's biggest advantage: interchangeable packs. With three batteries and a charger, you can run a fan through a multi-day outage by rotating packs whenever you have a way to recharge.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pick the fan that matches the platform you own, not the fan with the best specs. Ryobi ONE+ is the most common platform among homeowners and has a wide range of fans, from quiet personal models like the WhisperSeries to larger air-mover styles. DEWALT 20V MAX and Milwaukee M18 dominate among contractors and offer tougher, louder jobsite fans. Milwaukee M12 suits people who value compact, light tools and accept less runtime per pack.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not buy into a new platform just for a fan. The batteries and charger cost more than the fan, and you end up with a second set of packs that compete for charger time with the first. If you are not on any platform yet and want a fan, a built-in battery model is cheaper. If you plan to buy cordless tools anyway, choose the platform for the tools and let the fan follow.
          </p>

          <p>
            Battery care matters for emergency use. Lithium tool packs prefer to be stored partly charged, in a cool place, and they lose charge slowly over months. Before storm season, charge every pack fully and check that the charger still works. Keep at least one pack reserved for the fan and lights, so it is not dead in a drill when the power goes out. Avoid charging packs in a hot car or a very cold garage, where most chargers will refuse or slow down.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="usb-c" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Built-In Batteries and USB-C Charging
          </h2>

          <p>
            Built-in battery fans are the simplest answer for people without tool batteries. You charge the fan like a phone, and it runs cordless until the battery is empty. The best of them recharge over USB-C, which means the same cable and charger that serve your phone, laptop and power bank also serve the fan. In an outage, that standardization is a real advantage, because every USB-C power source you own can top it up.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check what the fan actually accepts. Some larger built-in battery fans still use a barrel-plug AC adapter rather than USB-C, which means you need the specific adapter and an AC outlet, or a power station with an inverter, to recharge. Fans that charge from USB-C can be fed directly from a power station's USB-C port, a <a href="/best-laptop-power-bank/">laptop power bank</a> or a solar charger, skipping the inverter and its losses entirely.
          </p>

          <p>
            The weakness of a built-in battery is that it is sealed. You cannot swap in a fresh pack at 2 a.m., and lithium cells slowly lose capacity over years of charge cycles and storage. A built-in battery fan that sat in a closet for two years may hold noticeably less than it did new. Charge it every few months during the off-season so it is full when you need it, and do not count on it alone for a long outage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch for exaggerated claims in this category. Battery capacity, airflow and runtime numbers on lesser-known brands are not always reliable, and runtime figures are almost always measured on the lowest speed. Buy from brands that publish battery capacity in watt-hours or milliamp-hours and that sell through retailers with real return policies. If a listing promises a full day of cooling from a pocket-size battery, it is measuring something very different from what you will experience.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power-station" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Running a Box Fan From a Power Station
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you own a power station, the cheapest way to cool a room in an outage is often an ordinary AC fan plugged into it. A 20-inch box fan moves far more air than any battery fan, and box fans of that size commonly draw well under 100 watts, so even a modest station can run one for hours. The label on the fan lists its power draw; that number, plus the station's capacity, is all you need to plan.
          </p>

          <p>
            Inverter overhead is the hidden cost. Converting battery power to household AC wastes some energy, and many stations draw a few watts or more just to keep the inverter on. For a 60-watt box fan, that overhead is a small fraction of the load. For a 5-watt USB fan, it can be larger than the fan itself, which is why small DC fans should run from the station's USB or 12V ports with the AC output switched off.
          </p>

          <p>
            Size the station to the night you need to cover. A 300-watt-hour unit runs a box fan for several hours, enough for the hottest part of the evening. A 1kWh station runs it all night with plenty left for phones and a router. If you are choosing a station for summer outages and camping, our guides to the <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-camping/">best power stations for camping</a> and the <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">best portable power stations overall</a> show how much each size covers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not expect a power station to run air conditioning for long. A window or portable air conditioner draws many times what a fan does, and even a large station only runs one for a few hours. Our guide to the <a href="/best-portable-air-conditioner-for-camping/">best portable air conditioners for camping</a> explains which compact units can run on batteries at all. For most outage nights, a fan, a cool shower and an open window after sunset do more for the money.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="outages" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Fans in a Summer Outage: What They Can and Cannot Do
          </h2>

          <p>
            A fan cools people by moving air across the skin, which speeds evaporation of sweat. It does not cool the air in the room. That works well in warm conditions, but the CDC notes that electric fans may not prevent heat-related illness once temperatures reach the high 90s, because blowing very hot air across the body can add heat rather than remove it. In that kind of heat, a fan alone is not enough.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use the fan alongside other cooling. Wet a cloth or a T-shirt and let the fan blow across it, take cool showers or sponge baths, and drink water steadily even if you are not thirsty. Keep blinds and curtains closed on sun-facing windows during the day. After sunset, when the outside air is cooler than inside, set a box fan in a window blowing out and open a window on the opposite side to pull cooler air through the house.
          </p>

          <p>
            Know the warning signs of heat exhaustion: heavy sweating, cold and clammy skin, nausea, dizziness, headache and weakness. Older adults, young children and people with chronic illnesses are at higher risk, as are people on medications that affect heat tolerance. If someone stops sweating, becomes confused or faints, that is a medical emergency. Call 911. During long, hot outages, a cooling center or a friend with power is the safer choice for anyone vulnerable. Medical devices come before fans; our <a href="/best-battery-backup-for-cpap/">CPAP battery backup guide</a> covers sizing for that.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fans are also useful in a winter outage, quietly. A small fan on its lowest speed can push warm air from a heater around a closed room so it does not pool at the ceiling. If you are planning for cold-weather outages as well as summer ones, our guide to the <a href="/best-indoor-heater-for-power-outage/">best indoor heaters for power outages</a> covers heaters that are rated for indoor use and the carbon monoxide safety that comes with them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="camp-jobsite" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Camping and Jobsite Use
          </h2>

          <p>
            At a campsite, weight and noise matter as much as airflow. A quiet personal fan hung from a tent loop or set on a camp table makes a hot night bearable, while a loud jobsite fan will annoy everyone in the next site. Tool-battery fans fit well if you also bring a cordless lantern or inflator on the same platform, so a couple of packs power everything and you only need one charger.
          </p>

          <p>
            Recharging at camp shapes the choice. If you have a vehicle, a 12V outlet or a small power station can refill tool packs and USB-C fans between outings. For longer trips without a vehicle, a USB-C fan paired with a solar power bank is the most self-sufficient setup. Car campers with bigger power needs may want a generator; our guide to the <a href="/best-generator-for-camping/">best generators for camping</a> covers quiet inverter models that fit campground rules.
          </p>

          <p>
            On a jobsite, toughness and battery sharing win. A fan that runs on the crew's existing packs gets used, while one that needs its own charger gets left in the truck. Larger jobsite fans with adjustable heads earn their place in attics, crawl spaces and freshly painted rooms, where moving air speeds drying and clears fumes. Keep fans out of the dust stream of saws and sanders, and blow fumes toward an open window or door rather than across the room.
          </p>

          <p>
            At home, a fan also helps long-term comfort in mild weather. If you are tired of running air conditioning in spring and fall, a <a href="/best-whole-house-fan/">whole house fan</a> pulls cool evening air through the entire house for a fraction of the power, though it needs grid power or a substantial backup system. Battery fans fill the gaps: the garage workbench, the patio, the bedroom on a night when the power is out.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="choosing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Which One to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you own Ryobi ONE+ batteries, buy the Ryobi WhisperSeries 7-1/2 inch fan. It is quiet enough to sleep beside, runs on packs you already have, and can run on AC power in normal use. It is the best battery powered fan for most homes simply because so many homes already have the batteries. Pair it with two charged packs and it covers most outage nights without help.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are on DEWALT 20V MAX and need airflow for a garage, attic or job site, buy the DEWALT 20V MAX Jobsite Fan. If you are on Milwaukee M12 and work in tight spaces, the M12 Compact Jobsite Fan is the natural fit. Do not switch platforms for a fan; buy the one that matches what you already own.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have no tool batteries, choose between a built-in battery floor fan such as the Geek Aire for room-scale cooling, and the O2COOL Treva 10-inch for an emergency kit that may sit for years. Add a Jisulife handheld fan for each family member as cheap personal cooling that charges from any USB-C source. These are the best small battery powered fans for anyone who wants simplicity over runtime.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you already own a power station, buy a Lasko 20-inch box fan and plug it in. It moves more air than anything else here for less money. And if you are building an outage kit from scratch, the fan is the easy part; start with power. Our guides to the <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">best portable power stations</a> and the <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">best home generators</a> will help you size the system that runs the fan, the fridge and the lights together.
          </p>
