        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Fan on a Battery Platform You Do Not Own</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A bare-tool fan looks cheap until you add a battery and charger, which often cost more than the fan. Buy the fan that matches the cordless tools already in your garage, or choose a built-in battery fan if you have none. Starting a second platform for one fan rarely makes sense.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting Runtime Claims at Face Value</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Runtime figures on fan boxes are usually measured on the lowest speed with the largest compatible battery. Work it out yourself: battery volts times amp-hours gives watt-hours, divided by the fan's power draw at the speed you will actually use. Plan on medium speed and you will rarely be disappointed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a Tiny USB Fan Through an Inverter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A power station's AC inverter uses power just to stay on, and for a 5-watt USB fan that overhead can exceed the fan's own draw. Plug small fans into the station's USB or 12V ports and switch the AC output off. Save the inverter for box fans and other AC loads.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing Rechargeable Fans Empty for Months</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Lithium batteries left fully drained for long periods can lose capacity or refuse to charge. Before storm season, charge every fan and tool pack, check that the charger works, and top them up every few months. For a kit that may sit for years, a D-cell fan with fresh batteries stored separately is more reliable.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Relying on a Fan Alone in Extreme Heat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fans cool people by evaporating sweat, and the CDC notes they may not prevent heat illness once temperatures reach the high 90s. Use a fan with wet cloths, cool showers, plenty of water and closed blinds. If anyone shows signs of heat illness, move them somewhere cooler and call 911 if symptoms are severe.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

