        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best battery powered fan for a power outage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes, a fan that runs on tool batteries you already own, such as the Ryobi ONE+ WhisperSeries, is the best choice because you can swap packs and run it for days. Without tool batteries, a built-in battery fan with USB-C charging is simpler, and a D-cell fan like the O2COOL Treva is best for kits that may sit unused for years.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a battery powered fan run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Divide the battery's watt-hours by the fan's power draw. An 18V 4Ah pack holds about 72 watt-hours, so a small fan drawing around 10 watts on low runs roughly six or seven hours, and less on high. Handheld fans with 5,000 milliamp-hour cells store about 18 watt-hours. Runtime claims on boxes usually assume the lowest speed.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run a box fan on a portable power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and it is one of the best uses of a power station in a summer outage. A 20-inch box fan commonly draws well under 100 watts; check the label for the exact figure. At around 60 watts, a 1kWh station runs it through a full night, and a 300Wh station runs it for several hours. Inverter overhead shortens runtime slightly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are tool battery fans worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are worth it if you already own the batteries. The fan costs little, packs swap in seconds, and a few charged batteries carry it through long outages. If you do not own the platform, the battery and charger add more cost than the fan itself, and a built-in battery fan or D-cell fan is usually the better buy.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do battery operated fans with D cells still make sense?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for emergency kits. Alkaline D cells keep most of their charge for years in storage, they are sold everywhere, and you can carry spares indefinitely. Rechargeable lithium fans may be flat or degraded after a long time on a shelf. Store the D cells outside the fan to avoid leaks, and replace them every few years.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a fan keep me safe in extreme heat without power?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not on its own. The CDC notes electric fans may not prevent heat-related illness when temperatures reach the high 90s. Use the fan with wet cloths, cool showers, steady water intake and closed blinds, and move to a cooler place if you can. Signs like confusion, fainting or stopping sweating are medical emergencies requiring 911.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What should I look for in a small battery powered fan?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Look for a published battery capacity in watt-hours or milliamp-hours, USB-C charging, a low speed quiet enough to sleep beside, and a stand or hook that lets you aim it. Avoid listings that promise all-day runtime from a pocket-size cell. For a bedside fan, quiet operation matters more than maximum airflow.
              </p>
